WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:15.871
When we watch a film or a play,

00:00:15.871 --> 00:00:16.996
we know that the actors

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:19.038
probably learned
their lines from a script,

00:00:19.038 --> 00:00:22.329
which essentially tells them
what to say and when to say it.

00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:26.246
A piece of written music
operates on exactly the same principle.

00:00:26.746 --> 00:00:28.079
In a very basic sense,

00:00:28.121 --> 00:00:31.163
it tells a performer what to play
and when to play it.

00:00:31.913 --> 00:00:34.412
Aesthetically speaking,
there's a world of difference

00:00:34.454 --> 00:00:37.371
between, say, Beethoven and Justin Bieber,

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:38.621
but both artists have used

00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:41.162
the same building blocks
to create their music:

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:42.204
notes.

00:00:42.246 --> 00:00:44.871
And although the end result
can sound quite complicated,

00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:48.412
the logic behind musical notes
is actually pretty straightforward.

00:00:48.454 --> 00:00:49.454
Let's take a look

00:00:49.454 --> 00:00:51.662
at the foundational
elements to music notation

00:00:51.662 --> 00:00:54.621
and how they interact
to create a work of art.

00:00:55.662 --> 00:00:59.329
Music is written on five parallel lines
that go across the page.

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:01.912
These five lines are called a staff,

00:01:01.912 --> 00:01:04.204
and a staff operates on two axes:

00:01:04.204 --> 00:01:05.496
up and down

00:01:05.496 --> 00:01:06.704
and left to right.

00:01:07.246 --> 00:01:09.163
The up-and-down axis tells the performer

00:01:09.204 --> 00:01:11.913
the pitch of the note
or what note to play,

00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:13.413
and the left-to-right axis

00:01:13.454 --> 00:01:15.538
tells the performer the rhythm of the note

00:01:15.538 --> 00:01:16.913
or when to play it.

00:01:17.413 --> 00:01:18.996
Let's start with pitch.

00:01:18.996 --> 00:01:21.038
To help us out,
we're going to use a piano,

00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:24.371
but this system works for pretty much
any instrument you can think of.

00:01:25.163 --> 00:01:26.788
In the Western music tradition,

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:30.204
pitches are named after
the first seven letters of the alphabet,

00:01:30.246 --> 00:01:31.704
A, B, C,

00:01:31.704 --> 00:01:33.663
D, E, F, and G.

00:01:34.454 --> 00:01:36.579
After that, the cycle repeats itself:

00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:38.621
A, B, C, D, E, F, G,

00:01:38.663 --> 00:01:39.996
A, B, C, D, E, F, G,

00:01:39.996 --> 00:01:41.079
and so on.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:43.663
But how do these pitches get their names?

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:46.246
Well, for example, if you played an F

00:01:46.288 --> 00:01:47.496
and then played another F

00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:49.204
higher or lower on the piano,

00:01:49.204 --> 00:01:51.246
you'd notice that they sound
pretty similar

00:01:51.288 --> 00:01:53.079
compared to, say, a B.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.954
Going back to the staff,

00:01:54.954 --> 00:01:57.663
every line and every space
between two lines

00:01:57.663 --> 00:01:59.329
represents a separate pitch.

00:02:00.038 --> 00:02:03.121
If we put a note on one of these lines
or one of these spaces,

00:02:03.121 --> 00:02:05.663
we're telling a performer
to play that pitch.

00:02:05.663 --> 00:02:07.996
The higher up on the staff
a note is placed,

00:02:08.038 --> 00:02:09.413
the higher the pitch.

00:02:09.413 --> 00:02:11.913
But there are obviously
many, many more pitches

00:02:11.913 --> 00:02:14.579
than the nine that these
lines and spaces gives us.

00:02:14.621 --> 00:02:16.121
A grand piano, for example,

00:02:16.163 --> 00:02:17.829
can play 88 separate notes.

00:02:18.288 --> 00:02:21.288
So how do we condense
88 notes onto a single staff?

00:02:21.788 --> 00:02:23.996
We use something called a clef,

00:02:23.996 --> 00:02:26.954
a weird-looking figure
placed at the beginning of the staff,

00:02:26.996 --> 00:02:28.663
which acts like a reference point,

00:02:28.704 --> 00:02:30.788
telling you that a particular
line or space

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:33.579
corresponds to a specific note
on your instrument.

00:02:33.621 --> 00:02:35.954
If we want to play notes
that aren't on the staff,

00:02:35.996 --> 00:02:38.121
we kind of cheat and draw
extra little lines

00:02:38.121 --> 00:02:39.329
called ledger lines

00:02:39.371 --> 00:02:41.079
and place the notes on them.

00:02:41.079 --> 00:02:44.454
If we have to draw so many ledger lines
that it gets confusing,

00:02:44.454 --> 00:02:46.746
then we need to change
to a different clef.

00:02:47.871 --> 00:02:50.204
As for telling a performer
when to play the notes,

00:02:50.246 --> 00:02:52.079
two main elements control this:

00:02:52.121 --> 00:02:54.038
the beat and the rhythm.

00:02:54.454 --> 00:02:56.038
The beat of a piece of music is,

00:02:56.079 --> 00:02:57.996
by itself, kind of boring.

00:02:58.038 --> 00:02:59.704
It sounds like this.

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:01.579
(Ticking)

00:03:02.163 --> 00:03:03.663
Notice that it doesn't change,

00:03:03.663 --> 00:03:05.954
it just plugs along quite happily.

00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:07.038
It can go slow

00:03:08.538 --> 00:03:09.621
or fast

00:03:10.496 --> 00:03:11.913
or whatever you like, really.

00:03:11.913 --> 00:03:14.579
The point is that just
like the second hand on a clock

00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:17.163
divides one minute into sixty seconds,

00:03:17.204 --> 00:03:20.163
with each second just as long
as every other second,

00:03:20.204 --> 00:03:21.954
the beat divides a piece of music

00:03:21.996 --> 00:03:25.079
into little fragments of time
that are all the same length:

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:26.121
beats.

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:28.538
With a steady beat as a foundation,

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:30.204
we can add rhythm to our pitches,

00:03:30.204 --> 00:03:32.371
and that's when music
really starts to happen.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:35.038
This is a quarter note.

00:03:35.038 --> 00:03:36.746
It's the most basic unit of rhythm,

00:03:36.788 --> 00:03:38.288
and it's worth one beat.

00:03:38.746 --> 00:03:41.288
This is a half note,
and it's worth two beats.

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:43.413
This whole note here is worth four beats,

00:03:43.413 --> 00:03:45.329
and these little guys are eighth notes,

00:03:45.371 --> 00:03:46.704
worth half a beat each.

00:03:47.413 --> 00:03:49.829
"Great," you say, "what does that mean?"

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.538
You might have noticed
that across the length of a staff,

00:03:52.538 --> 00:03:55.204
there are little lines dividing it
into small sections.

00:03:55.246 --> 00:03:56.913
These are bar lines

00:03:56.954 --> 00:03:59.371
and we refer to each section as a bar.

00:03:59.954 --> 00:04:01.704
At the beginning of a piece of music,

00:04:01.746 --> 00:04:02.829
just after the clef,

00:04:02.871 --> 00:04:04.954
is something called the time signature,

00:04:04.954 --> 00:04:07.996
which tells a performer
how many beats are in each bar.

00:04:07.996 --> 00:04:10.163
This says there are two beats in each bar,

00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:11.496
this says there are three,

00:04:11.538 --> 00:04:13.579
this one four, and so on.

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:15.871
The bottom number tells
us what kind of note

00:04:15.871 --> 00:04:18.329
is to be used as the basic
unit for the beat.

00:04:18.788 --> 00:04:20.496
One corresponds to a whole note,

00:04:20.496 --> 00:04:21.621
two to a half note,

00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:23.538
four to a quarter note,

00:04:23.579 --> 00:04:25.538
and eight to an eighth note, and so on.

00:04:25.954 --> 00:04:27.288
So this time signature here

00:04:27.329 --> 00:04:30.079
tells us that there are four
quarter notes in each bar,

00:04:30.121 --> 00:04:32.038
one, two, three, four;

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:33.621
one, two, three, four,

00:04:33.621 --> 00:04:34.704
and so on.

00:04:35.163 --> 00:04:36.329
But like I said before,

00:04:36.371 --> 00:04:37.788
if we just stick to the beat,

00:04:37.829 --> 00:04:39.371
it gets kind of boring,

00:04:39.371 --> 00:04:42.246
so we'll replace some quarter notes
with different rhythms.

00:04:42.913 --> 00:04:44.913
Notice that even though
the number of notes

00:04:44.913 --> 00:04:46.413
in each bar has changed,

00:04:46.413 --> 00:04:48.996
the total number of beats
in each bar hasn't.

00:04:50.121 --> 00:04:53.329
So, what does our musical
creation sound like?

00:04:53.371 --> 00:04:55.746
(Music)

00:04:55.746 --> 00:04:59.038
Eh, sounds okay, but maybe
a bit thin, right?

00:04:59.079 --> 00:05:01.954
Let's add another instrument
with its own pitch and rhythm.

00:05:02.413 --> 00:05:04.621
Now it's sounding like music.

00:05:05.788 --> 00:05:08.746
Sure, it takes some practice
to get used to reading it quickly

00:05:08.788 --> 00:05:10.788
and playing what we see on our instrument,

00:05:10.829 --> 00:05:12.579
but, with a bit of time and patience,

00:05:12.621 --> 00:05:14.371
you could be the next Beethoven

00:05:14.371 --> 00:05:16.496
or Justin Bieber.

